From 0ddc2ec21cc06cfdc92298e3224e49d66b2b6ad7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Karel Zak <kzak@redhat.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 09:22:44 +0200
Subject: lib/fileutils: add safe FD/openat2 helpers

Backport of the lib/fileutils infrastructure the libmount TOCTOU
hardening depends on.  No functional change on its own; nothing in this
branch calls the new helpers yet.

 * ul_openat_resolve() -- openat2(2) wrapper, returns ENOSYS when the
   syscall is not available.  configure.ac learns to probe for
   <linux/openat2.h>; RESOLVE_NO_SYMLINKS and RESOLVE_BENEATH get a
   fallback definition in include/fileutils.h for build environments
   with older kernel headers.

 * ul_open_no_symlinks() -- opens a path rejecting symlinks at any
   component, not just the last one.  Uses openat2(RESOLVE_NO_SYMLINKS)
   (Linux >= 5.6) and falls back to open(O_PATH) plus a readlink() of
   /proc/self/fd/<fd> when openat2() is unavailable.

 * ul_fd_mkpath() -- composes "/proc/self/fd/<fd>" on one place, with
   one buffer size (UL_FDPATH_BUFSIZ).

 * ul_fd_get_path() -- asks the kernel for the name of an already open
   file.  Refuses names which are not usable for open(): the
   "<path> (deleted)" names of unlinked files, the pseudo-names of
   pipes and sockets, and truncated readlink() results.  This is used
   to canonicalize the pinned mount target, so a silently shortened
   name would end up in the mount table and in utab.

Backport notes:

 * The patches are taken from the upstream stable/v2.42 branch; each of
   the commits referenced below records the corresponding master commit
   in its own message.  Five commits are squashed into one; fde56542c
   and 67fd3bf43 are fixups of the two commits before them.  This is the
   rhel/9 patch (4a4746991) reduced to what 2.32 needs.

 * openat2() is available on RHEL 8 since 8.7, so
   openat2(RESOLVE_NO_SYMLINKS) is the normal path on a current system.
   The older 8.x kernels answer with ENOSYS and
   open_no_symlinks_fallback() takes over: it opens the path with
   O_PATH, asks the kernel for the name of the result and refuses the
   file descriptor when the name differs from the requested path -- a
   symlink in any component, as well as a rename racing with the open,
   changes the name.  The name always belongs to the descriptor we
   return, so the check has no time-of-check-to-time-of-use window of
   its own.  It needs /proc.

   The fallback is entered on ENOSYS only.  A kernel or an LSM which
   answers openat2() with EPERM must not silently downgrade the
   protection.

 * ul_openat_resolve() needs SYS_openat2 from <sys/syscall.h> and
   struct open_how from <linux/openat2.h>, and a build root may have
   one without the other.  The openat2() branch is therefore guarded by
   both SYS_openat2 and HAVE_LINUX_OPENAT2_H; upstream tests
   SYS_openat2 only, which does not build where the header is missing.
   A binary built without the header uses the readlink() fallback
   everywhere -- the protection is kept, only the kernel side path
   resolution is not used.

 * ul_reopen() and ul_basename() do not exist in this branch, so the
   conversion of ul_reopen() to ul_fd_get_path() is not applicable.

 * The monitor and mount-API hunks of the upstream commits are dropped,
   this branch has neither.

 * The cosmetic conversion of term-utils/mesg.c to ul_fd_mkpath() is
   dropped as well; it is unrelated to the security fix and mesg(1) is
   not part of this backport.

 * ul_absolute_path(), ul_is_relative_path() and ul_endswith() are
   still called absolute_path(), is_relative_path() and endswith() here.

 * _PATH_PROC_FDDIR is new in include/pathnames.h; 2.32 has no
   definition for /proc/self/fd yet.

 * lib/fileutils.c now uses lib/canonicalize.c and lib/strutils.c, so
   test_fileutils has to link libcommon.la.  Upstream did this in the
   unrelated cleanup 1aff73db2; only the single line is taken.

 * ul_close_all_fds() and ul_copy_file() do not exist in 2.32 and the
   upstream context of the new code -- the xreaddir() based FD closing
   and the sendfile() based copy -- is not backported.  mkdir_p() keeps
   its name, upstream renamed it to ul_mkdir_p() later.

 * The TEST_PROGRAM_FILEUTILS block is moved to the end of the file (it
   was in the middle), gains an argument parser and the
   --open-no-symlinks test case.  "--fallback" makes the non-openat2()
   path testable on a machine with a new kernel:

	$ ln -s /tmp/r8t/real /tmp/r8t/link
	$ ./test_fileutils --open-no-symlinks /tmp/r8t/link
	test_fileutils: /tmp/r8t/link: Too many levels of symbolic links
	$ ./test_fileutils --open-no-symlinks /tmp/r8t/link --fallback
	test_fileutils: /tmp/r8t/link: Too many levels of symbolic links

